(AVParis 72.2.1) In whose kingdom the sun and moon are encircled [by halos] — those hostile peoples and provinces [are destined] for the other world.
(AVParis 72.2.2) Likewise, when crows, pigeons, herons, vultures, <s>Yakṣa</s>s, <s>Rākṣasa</s>s, <s>Piśāca</s>s, and wild beasts call out, respond, sing, or howl at night — at the tops of wheels, banners, houses, lodgings, and palaces;
(AVParis 72.2.3) When a tank, well, or reservoir erupts, rumbles, or flashes;
(AVParis 72.2.4) When meteors, embers, smoke, or flames appear upon chariots, machines, elephants, conveyances, and instruments;
(AVParis 72.2.5) [When there is] a deformed form in the <s>liṅga</s> — [it portends danger] for the king;
(AVParis 72.2.6) When an owl, chameleon, or hawk descends upon the royal umbrella; when a broken banner, or the king's staff, or the king's teeth [are damaged];

(AVParis 72.3.2) During a gathering of serpents, a violent windstorm, or the appearance and disappearance of water;
(AVParis 72.3.3) In the case of rainbows, twilight-glow, meteors, halos, lightning, pillars [of light], thunderbolts, <s>parigha</s> (iron-bar portent), <s>paridhi</s> (ring portent), and thunderclaps;
(AVParis 72.3.4) When it rains dust, hailstones, curds, honey, ghee, milk, marrow, or blood;
(AVParis 72.3.5) When the sun has diminished rays, [anomalies in] the two paths, the courses, loss of wealth, at the waning of the moon, at incomplete filling [of the moon], the appearances of decay, sudden burning and fumigation of the quarters from the latter part of the night;
(AVParis 72.3.6) Irregularity of planets, [their] rising, encroachment, mirage-cities, violent agitation of wind, separation of the moon from <s>tithi</s>s, <s>karaṇa</s>s, <s>muhūrta</s>s, <s>nakṣatra</s>s, planets, and the like;
(AVParis 72.3.7) Rivers flowing against their current; gatherings of crows on palaces, gateways, and banners; a wolf climbing onto a cart; excessive licking by a bull; counter-roaring of owls; hawks, vultures, and the like crying out at banners;
(AVParis 72.3.8) And distorted [beings], born of human and non-human origin; the ravings of women, children, and the elderly; when [the flame of] a blazing <s>Indra</s>-staff breaks at its base, [portents] in non-material things, when a single tree casts two shadows or a counter-shadow — [that which is] reversed;
(AVParis 72.3.9) Beyond that, shadows [appearing] without cause; when sacred trees or pillars fall, when [things that should] sprout do not sprout, when leaves fall from unfelled [branches] and dry-branched trees [appear]; when smoke, dust, or water appear and disappear in trees;
(AVParis 72.3.10) Much breaking of weapons; breaking of <s>Indra</s>-pillars, gate-towers, watchtowers, banners, and the like; when proper things are destroyed and improper things flourish; when firm things break;
(AVParis 72.3.11) When dry things sprout in a house, in an anthill, or in a sleeping place; at the arising of grass-tufts; at enmity with friends and friendliness with enemies; likewise when images of deities are broken;
(AVParis 72.3.12) When the king is displeased toward the citizens and toward servants and the like — on these occasions there are [the following] verses:
(AVParis 72.3.13) When the moon on the first day [of a fortnight] becomes distorted by anomaly, or undeveloped or cut off — one should foretell the death of the king.
